'\" t
.\" hydra(1) - adaptive multi-source file retriever
.\" Written for man(7): renders on groff (Linux) and mandoc (macOS, *BSD,
.\" illumos) alike. No GNU-only macros, no UTF-8 in the source.
.TH HYDRA 1 "2026-08-30" "hydra" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
hydra, hya \- multi-source file retriever with reassignable byte ranges
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hydra
.RI [ options ]
.IR URL ...
.br
.B hya
.RI [ options ]
.IR URL ...
.br
.B hydra interactive
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ URL ...]
.br
.B hydra checksum
.RI [ options ]
.IR URL ...
.br
.B hydra parity
.BR make | repair
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.br
.B hydra formats
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B hydra bench
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B hydra completions
.I SHELL
.br
.B hydra install\-completions
.RI [ SHELL ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B hydra compat\-link
.RI [ options ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hydra
retrieves an object over
.BR http ,
.BR https ,
or
.BR ftp ,
and keeps the work reassignable while it runs instead of committing to a
split up front.
.PP
The property it is built on is that a byte range is not a commitment.
An HTTP range request has a server-side start but a
.I client-side
end: to shrink a laggard's range you stop reading, with no message to the
server and no round trip.
A source that dies or throttles mid-transfer therefore does not strand its
share, which is what a static split cannot avoid.
FTP does not have this property \(em
.B REST
says where a transfer starts and nothing says where it ends \(em so FTP
transfers are single-source and sequential, and the cost is stated rather
than hidden.
See
.BR PROTOCOLS ,
below.
.PP
By default several URLs mean several
.IR files ,
matching
.BR wget (1)
and
.BR curl (1).
Pass
.B \-\-mirrors
to treat them as interchangeable copies of one object and assemble byte
ranges across them; that is only sound when every source provably serves
identical bytes, so sources that disagree on size or validator are dropped
with a reason printed.
.PP
Concurrency is
.I measured
rather than assumed.
With no
.B \-x
the client probes how many connections a path actually rewards and opens
that many; on a link already saturated by one connection, more connections
add no speed and none are opened.
Pass
.B \-\-no\-probe
to take
.B \-x
on faith.
.
.PP
A URL whose path ends in
.BR .m3u8 ", " .m3u
or
.B .mpd
is tried as an adaptive-stream manifest before anything else, as are all
URLs given with
.B \-\-inspect
or
.BR \-\-list\-streams .
HLS and DASH describe a ladder of renditions cut into hundreds of segments,
which is a different job from assembling reassignable ranges over one object.
The extension only decides whether the attempt is worth one small
.BR GET ;
the body settles it, so a
.B .m3u8
that answers with a login page costs that one request and then falls through
to an ordinary download.
See
.BR STREAMS .
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Output
.TP
\fB\-O\fI FILE\fR, \fB\-\-output\fI FILE\fR Write the object to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B \-\-remote\-name
Name the output from the URL's last path component
.RB ( curl " " \-O ).
.TP
.B \-\-content\-disposition
Name the output from a
.B Content\-Disposition
response header
.RB ( curl " " \-J ).
Accepted but not implemented in this release; see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
\fB\-P\fI DIR\fR, \fB\-\-output\-dir\fI DIR\fR Save into
.IR DIR .
The directory must exist unless
.B \-\-create\-dirs
is also given.
.TP
.B \-\-create\-dirs
Create missing parent directories of the output path.
.TP
.B \-\-stdout
Write the payload to standard output.
Everything human moves to standard error, so the stream stays a single clean
object.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-save ", " \-\-discard
Run the transfer but discard the bytes.
Size, digest, format and timings are still measured and reported.
Writes nothing at any point, including no resume sidecar, and so works in a
directory with no write permission.
Combine with
.B \-\-json
for a machine-readable probe.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-clobber
Skip the download entirely if the output file already exists
.RB ( wget " " \-nc ).
The existing file is left byte-for-byte untouched.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite an existing output file without asking.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-sort\-by\-type
File the finished object into a per-category subdirectory
.RB ( Video ", " Music ", " Compressed ", " Documents ", " Programs ", ...),"
the way IDM does.
The category comes from the payload's magic bytes, not from the extension or
the server's
.BR Content\-Type ,
both of which are frequently wrong.
The full mapping is in
.BR hydra\-formats (1).
.PP
When the output file already exists and neither
.B \-\-force
nor
.B \-\-no\-clobber
was given, an interactive run asks what to do
.RB ( c
continue,
.B r
start over,
.B s
skip).
A non-interactive run \(em stdin closed, piped, or at EOF \(em never
destroys the existing file: it writes to the first free name in the
\fIpath\fR, \fIpath\fB.1\fR, \fIpath\fB.2\fR series, bounded at 9999.
.
.SS Transfer control
.TP
\fB\-x\fI N\fR, \fB\-\-max\-connection\-per\-server\fI N\fR Connections per
source.
Omit to measure the useful number.
.TP
\fB\-s\fI N\fR, \fB\-\-split\fI N\fR Alias for
.BR \-x ,
for
.BR aria2c (1)
muscle memory.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-total\-connections " N"
Total connections across all hosts.
Default 16.
This is an
.I aggregate
ceiling: splitting a transfer across mirrors does not multiply it, so
.B \-\-max\-total\-connections 2
with
.B \-x 8
over two hosts opens two sockets in total, not eight per host.
The per-host limit still binds independently, and one connection is always
available.
.TP
.B \-\-polite
One connection per host, for volunteer mirrors.
Overrides an ambitious
.BR \-x .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-probe
Do not measure the useful concurrency; open what
.B \-x
asked for.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit\-rate " RATE"
Cap the aggregate transfer rate, e.g.\&
.BR 500k ,
.BR 2M .
An unparsable value is a hard error, not a silent fallback to unlimited: a
user who asked for a cap and did not get one may saturate a metered link.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-continue
Continue a partially-downloaded file.
Resume is refused, with the reason printed, when the object has changed
underneath the partial copy \(em see
.B FILES
for the sidecar that makes that check possible.
.TP
\fB\-r\fI RANGE\fR, \fB\-\-range\fI RANGE\fR Retrieve only this byte range:
.BR 0\-1023 ,
.BR 1024\- ,
or
.B \-512
for the last 512 bytes.
The delivered file is the span itself, at offset 0 \(em not the span written
into a file of the object's full extent.
.TP
.BI \-\-start\-pos " OFFSET"
Begin at this byte offset
.RB ( wget " " \-\-start\-pos ", " curl " " "\-C N" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-filesize " BYTES"
Refuse an object larger than this.
.TP
\fB\-t\fI N\fR, \fB\-\-tries\fI N\fR Retries per range before giving up on a
source.
Default 4.
.TP
.BI \-\-retry\-delay " SECS"
Seconds between retries.
Default 0.5.
Accepted but not implemented in this release; see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
\fB\-T\fI SECS\fR, \fB\-\-timeout\fI SECS\fR Per-request timeout.
Default 30.
.TP
.BI \-\-connect\-timeout " SECS"
Connection timeout.
Accepted but not implemented in this release; see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
.BI \-\-wait " SECS"
Seconds to wait between separate URLs.
Default 0.
.
.SS Several URLs
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " MODE"
How to treat several URLs.
.B same
(default) fetches them concurrently as separate files;
.B queue
fetches them one after another.
Conflicts with
.BR \-\-mirrors .
.TP
.B \-\-mirrors
Treat every URL as a mirror of
.I one
object and assemble byte ranges across them.
Requires that all sources agree on size and on a strong validator.
.TP
.BR \-Z ", " \-\-parallel
Retrieve several URLs concurrently
.RB ( curl " " \-Z ).
Distinct objects, not mirrors.
.TP
.BI \-\-parallel\-max " N"
Maximum concurrent transfers under
.BR \-\-parallel .
Default 4.
Accepted but not implemented in this release; see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
.BI \-\-input\-file " FILE"
Read URLs from
.IR FILE ,
one per line.
There is deliberately no
.B \-i
short form in native mode:
.B \-i
is boolean here (see
.BR COMPATIBILITY ),
so a value-taking
.B \-i
would make
.B \-iv
parse
.B v
as a filename.
.
.SS Adaptive streams
.TP
.BI \-\-quality " HEIGHT"
Preferred rendition height, e.g.\&
.BR "\-\-quality 720" .
An exact match wins; failing that the nearest rendition
.I below
the request is taken, and only when every rendition is larger than the
request is the smallest of them used.
Never a larger one by surprise \(em asking for 720 on a ladder that offers
only 1080p and 2160p gives 1080p, not 2160p.
Without it the first rendition the manifest lists is used, which is the
publisher's own default.
.TP
.BI \-\-container " FMT"
Container for the finished file:
.B mp4
(default) or
.BR ts .
MPEG-TS segments become MP4 through a system
.BR ffmpeg (1);
with no ffmpeg on this machine, ask for
.B ts
and keep the assembled transport stream.
Any other value is treated as
.BR mp4 ;
see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
.B \-\-list\-streams
Print the renditions a manifest offers, then exit.
A manifest is a few kilobytes, so this is the cheap way to see what
.B \-\-quality
is choosing between.
The listing goes to standard output: the protocol and URL, then one indented
line per rendition
.RB ( "720p  1400 kbps  avc1.64001f" ),
and for DASH the audio representations after the video ones.
A URL that turns out not to be a manifest is a failed premise, not an
invitation to download it: the run exits 1 having written nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-inspect
Report what the server says about a URL, then exit.
No body is transferred either way.
For a manifest this is the same rendition listing
.B \-\-list\-streams
prints; for an ordinary object it is the URL after redirects (each hop
printed as it is followed), the file name the server implies, the size, the
content type, the extension, whether byte ranges are accepted \(em that is,
whether a lost connection costs the whole transfer \(em the validator, the
modification time rendered in
.I local
time, and any
.B Content\-Disposition.
A URL that is not a manifest is an ordinary answer here, which is the whole
difference from
.BR \-\-list\-streams .
.TP
.BI \-\-record\-seconds " SECONDS"
Record a live stream for this much media, then finish the file.
The budget is spent in the
.I media
the manifest declares per segment, not on the wall clock: a DVR window is
drained far faster than real time, so a wall-clock timer would hand back
hours of it for a "60 seconds" ask.
Without this flag a live recording runs until the broadcast ends or Ctrl-C
stops it; either way the result is a finished, playable file.
.PP
.B \-x
means segments in flight for a stream, exactly as it means connections per
source for a file: 8 by default, capped at 32.
.B \-\-limit\-rate
caps the whole transfer rather than each segment.
.B \-\-adaptive
tunes ranged file downloads and has no meaning for segments; a run that
passes it against a stream is told so rather than having it silently dropped.
.PP
.B \-\-inspect
and
.B \-\-list\-streams
ask a question about
.I one
object, so they refuse rather than be dropped: combining either with
.BR \-\-spider ", " \-\-stdout ", " \-\-no\-save ", " \-\-checksum ", " \-\-range
or
.BR \-\-start\-pos ,
or passing several URLs without
.BR \-\-mirrors ,
is a usage error (exit 2).
The same list governs auto-detection, quietly: with any of those flags in
play a manifest URL is fetched as the ordinary file it also is.
.
.SH INTEGRITY
.TP
.BI \-\-checksum " HASH"
Verify the finished file, e.g.\&
.BR "\-\-checksum sha256:abc..." .
Accepts
.B sha256:
followed by 64 hex digits, or the bare digest.
A mismatch is reported and exits 1.
An unparsable value is a
.I usage
error and exits 2 at argument parsing, rather than surviving to the comparison
and being reported as wrong bytes \(em the exit code was right either way, but
blaming the server for a malformed argument is the more expensive mistake.
.TP
.BI \-\-emit\-manifest " FILE"
Write a per-chunk digest manifest for what arrived.
The manifest localizes future corruption to a single chunk, which turns a
whole-file re-download into one chunk refetch.
It costs about 0.012% of the object at the default 4 MiB grid.
Format:
.IR docs/INTEGRITY.md .
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk\-digests " FILE"
Verify each chunk against a manifest as it arrives, refetching mismatches.
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk\-size " BYTES"
Chunk grid for
.BR \-\-emit\-manifest .
Default 4 MiB.
.TP
.BI \-\-etag\-compare " FILE"
Compare against an ETag stored in
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BI \-\-etag\-save " FILE"
Save the object's ETag to
.IR FILE .
.PP
A manifest read from a
.I local path
is trusted and may supply erasure positions for a repair.
One fetched from the object's own origin may only
.IR detect ,
never repair: erasure decoding trusts its inputs completely, so positions
chosen by whoever served the bytes would hand them control of the output.
A refetch driven by an advertised manifest is self-correcting, because the
refetched chunk is checked again; a repair is not.
See
.IR docs/INTEGRITY.md " \(sc1"
and
.BR hydra\-parity (1).
.
.SH REPORTING
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress all output except errors.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verbose
Reduce output without silencing it
.RB ( wget " " \-nv ).
Accepted but not implemented in this release; see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Verbose.
Repeat
.RB ( \-vv )
for scheduler-level detail.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the result as JSON on standard output, and nothing else.
Implies quiet: a machine-readable result must be the only thing on the
stream or it cannot be piped to a parser.
See
.B JSON OUTPUT
below.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-progress
Suppress the live progress display but keep summaries.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-progress
Force the progress display on even when stdout is not a terminal.
Accepted but not implemented in this release; see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-i ", " \-\-server\-response ", " \-\-include
Print the request and response headers.
Works both on a real transfer and under
.BR \-\-spider .
Not
.BR \-I :
in curl that means "headers only, no body", which here is
.BR \-\-spider .
.TP
.B \-\-spider
Probe headers and report without retrieving the body
.RB ( wget " " \-\-spider ", " curl " " \-I ).
Writes no file.
.TP
.B \-\-fail
Exit non-zero on an HTTP error
.RB ( curl " " \-f ).
Note that in this release the error body is still written to the output path
before the non-zero exit, where curl's
.B \-f
writes nothing.
Check the exit status; do not read the absence of a file as the signal.
See
.BR BUGS .
.TP
.B \-\-show\-error
Show errors even when quiet.
The failure reason goes to stderr under
.BR \-q ,
as curl's
.B \-sS
pair does; without it a quiet run that failed is indistinguishable from one
that succeeded except by its exit status.
.TP
\fB\-o\fI FILE\fR, \fB\-\-logfile\fI FILE\fR Log to
.I FILE
instead of the terminal, truncating it.
The animated progress frame is suppressed and no ANSI escapes are written, so
the file is greppable; the summary line, verbose events and post-transfer
notes all land there.
.TP
.BI \-\-logfile\-append " FILE"
Append to
.I FILE
instead of truncating.
.
.SH NETWORK
.TP
\fB\-H\fI LINE\fR, \fB\-\-header\fI LINE\fR With a value
.RB ( "\-H 'Name: value'" ),
send that request header.
With
.I no
value \(em
.B \-H Name
or
.B "\-H Name:"
\(em ask what the server answered with: the value is printed and the program
exits without transferring the body.
A trailing colon is punctuation, not a value, so both spellings mean the
same thing.
.IP
Header arguments are validated during parsing, not at send time, because
they are written onto the wire verbatim: a malformed one produces a request
the server never answers, which presents to the user as a hang.
A field-name must be an RFC 9110 token, and a CR or LF anywhere is refused
as header injection rather than as a typo.
.IP
The cost of the query spelling is that an intentionally empty request header
(curl's
.BR "\-H 'Name;'" )
cannot be sent.
.TP
\fB\-U\fI STRING\fR, \fB\-A\fI STRING\fR, \fB\-\-user\-agent\fI STRING\fR
User-Agent to send.
Default
.BR hydra/0.1 .
.TP
.BI \-\-proxy " URL"
Proxy to use
.RB ( curl " " \-x ).
Accepts
.BR http:// ,
.BR socks4:// ,
.BR socks4a:// ,
.BR socks5:// ,
and
.BR socks5h:// ,
with optional
.BI user:pass@
credentials.
SOCKS is a different mechanism, not a different spelling: an HTTP proxy
rewrites requests or opens a
.B CONNECT
tunnel, while SOCKS forwards a raw TCP stream and lets the proxy resolve DNS
\(em which is what
.B "ssh \-D"
and Tor expose, and the only option when local DNS cannot resolve the origin
at all.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-proxy
Ignore any proxy in the environment.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-location
Follow redirects.
On by default; the flag exists so scripts can be explicit.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-redirs " N"
Maximum redirects to follow.
Default 8.
.B 0
refuses to follow any redirect, naming the location it declined; exhausting
the budget fails with the flag named.
The limit applies to the transfer and the header-report path alike.
.TP
.B \-\-insecure
Allow certificates that do not verify
.RB ( curl " " \-k ", " wget " " \-\-no\-check\-certificate ).
.TP
.BR \-4 ", " \-\-ipv4
Resolve names to IPv4 only.
A host with no address of the requested family is an
.IR error ,
never a silent fallback to the family excluded \(em refusing is what the flag
is for.
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-ipv6
Resolve names to IPv6 only.
Conflicts with
.BR \-4 :
the combination has no satisfiable reading, so it is rejected rather than
resolved in favour of one of them.
.PP
Through a proxy the restriction applies to the hop this client opens, which is
the one to the proxy; beyond a SOCKS proxy the origin is resolved by the proxy
and this client has no say.
.
.SH GENERAL
.TP
.BI \-\-compat " DIALECT"
Command-line dialect:
.BR native ", " wget ", or " curl .
Also inferred from the name the binary is invoked as.
See
.BR COMPATIBILITY .
.TP
.BI \-\-queue\-file " FILE"
Queue file for interactive mode.
See
.BR hydra\-interactive (1).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.B \-h
gives a summary,
.B \-\-help
the long form.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.
.SH PROTOCOLS
.TS
tab(@) box;
lb lb lb lb
l  l  l  l .
Scheme@Ranged@Preempt cost@Validator
_
http, https@yes, client-bounded@0 RTT@strong ETag
ftp@yes (REST)@2 RTT@none usable
.TE
.PP
.B http
and
.B https
are the case the scheduler is designed for: shrinking a range costs nothing,
so work is reassignable at any moment.
.PP
.B ftp
supports ranged reads through
.BR REST ,
but nothing says where a transfer
.I ends
\(em
.B RETR
runs to end-of-file \(em so shrinking a range means
.BR ABOR ,
draining the reply, and a fresh
.B PASV
for the next one: two control round trips where HTTP pays zero.
Two consequences, both deliberate.
FTP transfers are
.B single-source
\(em
.B SIZE
plus
.B MDTM
cannot prove two mirrors serve identical bytes, and a file spliced from two
versions passes every length check while being silently wrong \(em and
.BR sequential ,
since with preemption costing round trips there is nothing to gain from
splitting one source.
Resume still works, because
.B REST
is a ranged read.
A server without
.B SIZE
and
.B REST
(both RFC 3659 extensions, not RFC 959) is refused rather than guessed at.
.PP
FTP authentication is part of the URL \(em
.BI ftp:// user : pass @ host / path
\(em unlike HTTP where it is a header.
Credentials are percent-decoded, split on the
.I last
.RB ` @ '
so a password may contain one, and kept out of debug output, error messages,
and the exchange that
.B \-S
prints.
HTTP URLs carrying userinfo discard it: it never reaches an endpoint, a
request line, or a proxy
.BR CONNECT .
.PP
.B sftp://
and
.B scp://
are recognised by name so the refusal can say
.I why
rather than "unsupported scheme".
SFTP is the best fit of any protocol for this scheduler and is unbuilt only
because it needs an SSH transport; SCP streams a whole file with no offsets,
so there are no ranges to schedule and it will not be built.
.
.SH STREAMS
HLS
.RB ( .m3u8 )
and DASH
.RB ( .mpd )
manifests are handled by a separate path: a manifest is not a file, so it
cannot go through the range scheduler at all.
.PP
A master playlist is read for its ladder, one rendition is chosen (see
.BR \-\-quality ),
and its media playlist is fetched and turned into a plan \(em after which no
further playlist is read.
DASH carries video and audio as separate representations, so both are
planned and the two finished tracks are combined at the end.
Relative URIs resolve against the address the manifest
.I finally
came from, not the address asked for: a CDN answering with a redirect to a
regional edge is the common case, and up to five hops are followed both for
the manifest and, independently, for each segment \(em carrying the segment's
byte range with it, so a playlist that carves every segment out of one file
does not fetch that whole file per segment.
.PP
Progress is a single line on standard error, so standard output stays clean;
.BR \-q ", " \-\-json
and
.B \-\-no\-progress
each silence it.
.
.SS Naming the output
.B \-O
and
.B \-P
mean what they always mean.
Without
.BR \-O ,
the name comes from the manifest's path with the container's extension
appended \(em except that manifests are almost always called something
generic, so a last component of
.BR index ", " master ", " manifest ", " playlist ", " mono ", " stream ,
.BR media ", " video " or " main
is skipped in favour of the directory above it, which is nearly always the
asset id.
.
.SS Containers and ffmpeg
Segments are assembled first and converted afterwards, and what is possible
depends on what the segments are:
.TS
tab(@) box;
lb lb lb lb
l  l  l  l .
Segments@Asked@With ffmpeg@Without
_
MPEG-TS@ts@placed as assembled@placed as assembled
MPEG-TS@mp4@remuxed@refused
fragmented MP4@mp4@remuxed, faststart@kept as assembled
fragmented MP4@ts@remuxed@refused
DASH video+audio@mp4@combined@refused
.TE
.PP
The remux is always a stream copy, never a re-encode.
For fragmented MP4 it is an improvement rather than a requirement \(em the
assembly already plays \(em so when ffmpeg declines, the assembled file is
kept and the reason is printed.
For MPEG-TS to MP4 the thing asked for genuinely cannot be produced, so that
is an error instead: the transfer exits 1 and the staged
.IB output .part.t N
files are left in place, playable on their own, rather than deleted because
the last step could not run.
.PP
ffmpeg is found on
.B PATH
and, on a desktop where
.B PATH
is minimal, in the usual install directories
.RI ( /opt/homebrew/bin ", " /usr/local/bin ", " "C:\(rsProgram Files\(rsffmpeg\(rsbin" ,
and so on).
.
.SS Encryption and DRM
HLS AES-128 is ordinary content protection, not DRM: the playlist names a
key URL and it is fetched over the same session as everything else, so those
streams download normally and the key count is reported.
Anything else is refused before a byte is written.
A manifest declaring Widevine, PlayReady, FairPlay or MPEG common encryption
is refused as the boundary it is \(em hydra does not bypass the technical
measures that protect audio and video content \(em and
.B SAMPLE\-AES
and its relatives are refused as not implemented.
Live streams under any encryption are refused too: keys rotate mid-broadcast
and appending ciphertext would "succeed" over a file of noise.
.
.SS Live recording
A live manifest publishes a sliding window and has no end of its own, so it
is recorded rather than downloaded: the window is refetched at the interval
the manifest implies, new segments are appended in playlist order, and the
file is finished when the broadcast ends, when
.B \-\-record\-seconds
is spent, or on Ctrl-C.
Stopping a recording is the end of it, not an interruption; the file is
complete and playable.
.PP
An encoder that restarts renumbers its segments, which is detected and
followed rather than being seen as segments already taken.
A segment that cannot be fetched is a gap the recording moves past; a
manifest that publishes nothing at all three refreshes running is an error.
A recording keeps no checkpoint \(em there is nothing to resume into once the
broadcast has moved on \(em so, unlike a VOD assembly, it cannot be continued
later.
.
.SS Resuming a VOD assembly
Each track is assembled into
.IB output .part.t N
beside a checkpoint sidecar
.IB output .part.t N .ck
recording how many segments and bytes are already in it.
Ctrl-C during an assembly exits 1 and says which staging file was kept;
running the same command again reuses it when the plan still matches, and
starts over when it does not.
Both are removed once the finished file is in place.
.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B wget
and
.B curl
assign incompatible meanings to 15 of 19 commonly-used short flags, so no
single namespace can be compatible with both.
.PP
Native mode resolves each letter by one rule: whichever tool's meaning is
expressible in a downloader that does not crawl and does not upload wins the
letter.
.TS
tab(@) box;
lb lb lb lb
l  l  l  l .
Flag@wget@curl@native
_
\-O@output file@remote\-name@wget
\-o@log file@output file@wget
\-c@continue@cookie\-jar@wget
\-q@quiet@no .curlrc@wget
\-H@span\-hosts@header@curl
\-U@user\-agent@proxy\-user@wget
\-A@accept list@user\-agent@curl
\-t@tries@telnet\-option@wget
\-T@timeout@upload\-file@wget
\-r@recursive@byte range@curl
\-L@relative\-only@follow redirects@curl
\-i@input file@include headers@curl
\-N@timestamping@no\-buffer@wget
\-P@directory\-prefix@ftp\-port@wget
\-x@force\-directories@proxy@curl
.TE
.PP
A second criterion overrides the first when they disagree: a short flag that
takes a value cannot be clustered, so when one tool's meaning is boolean and
the other's takes a value, the boolean wins.
.B \-i
is the case that proves it \(em with wget's meaning,
.B "hydra \-iv URL"
read
.B v
as an input filename and failed with a message about
.BR \-\-input\-file ,
which is a silent misinterpretation rather than an error about the flag.
.PP
For exact fidelity to either tool, select a dialect:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-compat=wget \-O out.bin URL
hydra \-\-compat=curl \-o out.bin URL
.fi
.RE
.PP
or install the binary under the other tool's name, after which existing
scripts work unchanged:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra compat\-link \-\-dry\-run
hydra compat\-link
.fi
.RE
.PP
That places
.BR wget " and " curl
links beside the binary and reports, per name, whether typing it will actually
reach hydra.
It has to: the dialect comes from the name the binary is invoked as, so a link
is only reached from a directory that is on
.B $PATH
.I before
the directory holding the real
.BR curl / wget .
A hand-written
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
ln \-s hydra curl
.fi
.RE
.PP
does the same thing, but in the current directory, where it is reached as
.B ./curl
and nothing else \(em which is the usual reason it appears not to work.
Use
.B \-\-dir
to place the links elsewhere,
.B \-\-name
to choose the names, and
.B \-\-force
to replace an existing file (never done otherwise: the point is not to clobber
a system
.BR curl ).
.PP
The names
.BR hydra\-wget ", " hydra\-curl ", " hydra_wget ", and " hydra_curl
are recognised too, as is a
.B .exe
suffix; those are the safe choice when the real tools must keep their names.
Precedence is:
.B \-\-compat
on the command line, then the invoked name, then native.
.PP
Under a dialect, a repeated boolean flag is accepted rather than rejected \(em
.B wget \-q \-q
and
.B curl \-s \-sS
run, as they do under the real tools; the repeat is redundant, not an error.
.B \-v
still counts, so
.B \-vv
is a level.
.PP
Under a dialect, a flag hydra cannot honour is
.B refused with a reason,
never ignored.
A silently-ignored
.B \-\-limit\-rate
can saturate a metered link; a silently-ignored
.B \-\-post\-data
sends a request the user did not ask for.
Refused categories include recursive crawling
.RB ( \-r ", " \-\-mirror ", " \-\-accept ", " \-\-span\-hosts "),"
request bodies and non-GET methods
.RB ( \-\-post\-data ", " \-\-form ", " \-X "),"
cookies, authentication
.RB ( \-\-user ", " \-\-netrc ", " \-\-oauth2\-bearer "),"
WARC archiving, and content encoding
.RB ( \-\-compressed ).
That last one is refused on a structural ground rather than an effort one: a
compressed body cannot be assembled from byte ranges fetched in parallel,
because the ranges are of the
.I compressed
stream and the client cannot know where they fall in the decoded object.
Only flags that are genuinely inert for a downloader \(em
.BR \-\-no\-dns\-cache ,
.B \-\-tcp\-nodelay
\(em are accepted and dropped, and each is listed explicitly rather than
matched by a catch-all; at
.B \-v
they are reported as accepted with no effect.
.PP
This refusal discipline applies to the dialects only.
Native mode has its own inert flags, which are
.I not
refused; they are listed in
.BR BUGS .
.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
.B \-\-json
prints one pretty-printed document and nothing else, so
.B "hydra \-\-json URL | jq .size"
works.
A single-URL run emits an object with these fields:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
url               requested URL
output            path written, or the discard sink
size              bytes delivered
elapsed_s         wall time for the whole invocation
transfer_s        wall time for the byte transfer alone
setup_s           wall time spent probing beforehand
throughput_bps    bytes per second
requests          range requests issued
connections       connections actually used
delta_s           scheduler tick
sha256            digest of what arrived, if computed
checksum_ok       true/false against \-\-checksum, else null
resumed_from      bytes already present at start
ok                whether the transfer succeeded
note              human note, if any
format            detected format, e.g. "gzip"
category          detected category, e.g. "archive"
format_conflict   payload vs. name vs. Content\-Type disagreement
format_label      short label, e.g. "gzip\-compressed tar"
format_description    one sentence about the format
category_description  what the category is and what to do with it
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I elapsed_s
and
.I transfer_s
are reported separately on purpose: they differ by seconds on a slow path,
and a single figure next to a progress bar reading "1.7s" looks like two
clocks disagreeing.
Setup is a per-path cost a real client caches; transfer is the steady-state
figure.
.PP
A multi-file run wraps them:
.B files
is the array of per-file objects above,
.B count
their number, and
.B ok
whether every one succeeded.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
Every requested transfer completed and any checksum verified.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure: a transfer failed, a checksum mismatched, TLS setup failed,
or an HTTP error status was returned.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unrecognised or malformed argument, an unparsable
.B \-\-limit\-rate
or
.BR \-\-range ,
no URL given, an unreadable
.BR \-\-input\-file ,
an unknown
.B bench
harness, or a flag refused by the active compatibility dialect.
.PP
The distinction is load-bearing for scripts: exit 2 means the command was
wrong and retrying it verbatim cannot help, while exit 1 means the command
was right and the network or the server was not.
.PP
Streams follow the same rule.
.B \-\-list\-streams
on a URL that is not a manifest exits 1; a refused stream (DRM, an
unsupported encryption, a live playlist that published nothing) exits 1; an
interrupted assembly exits 1 with its staging files kept; and combining
.B \-\-inspect
or
.B \-\-list\-streams
with a flag that contradicts it exits 2.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR http_proxy ", " HTTP_PROXY
Proxy to use when
.B \-\-proxy
is not given, checked in that order.
Only these two are read; the value is parsed as
.RI [ scheme ://] host : port
and a port is required.
.B \-\-no\-proxy
suppresses them.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Base directory for the interactive queue file.
Falls back to
.IR $HOME/.local/state ,
then to the current directory.
.TP
.B HOME
Used for the queue path when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset.
.PP
Note that the
.B no_proxy
environment variable is
.I not
consulted; per-host proxy exemption must be expressed with the
.B \-\-no\-proxy
flag.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB output .hydra
Resume sidecar, written beside the output file as JSON: the object's size,
the validator it was served with
.RB ( ETag ,
else
.BR Last\-Modified ),
the byte ranges already written, and the URL.
Resume compares the validator before reusing a single byte, because resuming
across a server-side update would splice two versions of a file together
\(em a failure that produces a file of exactly the right length.
Removed on successful completion, and never created under
.BR \-\-no\-save .
.TP
.IB output .hydra.tmp
Transient.
The sidecar is written here and atomically renamed, so a crash mid-write
cannot leave a sidecar describing a state the file was never in.
.TP
\fIoutput\fB.1\fR, \fIoutput\fB.2\fR, ...
Non-destructive alternate output names, used when the target exists and the
run is not interactive. wget's convention, bounded at 9999.
.TP
.IB output .part.t N
Staging file for stream track
.IR N :
the segments as assembled, before the container step.
Kept when the run is interrupted, so the next run can continue it, and kept
when the final conversion fails, because it is playable on its own.
.TP
.IB output .part.t N .ck
Checkpoint for that staging file: the segments and bytes already written.
A VOD assembly resumes from it when the plan still matches; a live recording
writes none.
.TP
.IB output .part.s N .\|.\|. ", " output .part.i N
Transient per-segment and init-segment staging files, removed as each one is
appended.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/hydra/queue.json
Interactive queue state.
See
.BR hydra\-interactive (1).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/hydra/queue.log
Log written by a backgrounded headless worker, beside its queue file.
.TP
.IB FILE .parity
Default output of
.BR "hydra parity make" .
See
.BR hydra\-parity (1).
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Retrieve an object, measuring the useful concurrency:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra https://example.com/big.iso
.fi
.RE
.PP
Two replicas of one object, four connections each, into a named file:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-mirrors \-O out.iso \-x 4 \e
      https://a.example/f https://b.example/f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Resume, capped at 2 MB/s:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-c \-\-limit\-rate 2M https://example.com/big.iso
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask what a server answers with, without transferring the body:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-H ETag https://example.com/f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check what a URL really serves, leaving nothing behind:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-no\-save \-\-json https://example.com/f | jq '.sha256, .format'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipe the payload while keeping the stream clean:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-stdout https://example.com/f.tar.gz | tar xz
.fi
.RE
.PP
Download and record a chunk manifest, so later corruption costs one chunk
rather than the whole object:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-emit\-manifest f.manifest.json \-O f.bin https://example.com/f.bin
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what a stream offers before fetching any of it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-list\-streams https://cdn.example/asset/master.m3u8
.fi
.RE
.PP
Take the 720p rendition, or the nearest below it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-quality 720 https://cdn.example/asset/master.m3u8
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep the transport stream instead of remuxing, on a machine with no ffmpeg:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-container ts \-O talk.ts https://cdn.example/asset/index.m3u8
.fi
.RE
.PP
Record two minutes of a live broadcast and finish the file:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-record\-seconds 120 https://live.example/channel.m3u8
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask what a URL is, whatever it turns out to be:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-inspect https://example.com/f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Install tab completion for the current shell:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra install\-completions
.fi
.RE
.PP
Install the
.BR wget / curl
entry points and check whether the shell will reach them:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra compat\-link \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.
.SH NOTES
.B \-\-json
implies quiet, and
.B \-\-stdout
moves all human output to standard error.
Both exist so the payload channel carries exactly one thing; do not expect
progress on stdout when either is in effect.
.PP
In multi-file mode, output names are derived from each URL's last path
component and are
.I not
deduplicated against each other.
Two URLs whose basenames collide \(em
.I .../4.17.21/package.json
and
.IR .../4.17.20/package.json ,
say \(em both resolve to
.IR package.json ,
and the run reports two successful transfers while leaving one file on disk.
Fetch colliding names into separate
.B \-\-output\-dir
runs, or one at a time with
.BR \-O .
.PP
.B \-\-output\-dir
does not imply
.BR \-\-create\-dirs :
a missing directory is an error, not something created for you.
.PP
Under
.B \-\-mirrors
a source that cannot be proven identical to the others is dropped with its
reason printed, rather than mixed in.
Two mirrors agreeing on
.B Content\-Length
while serving different builds is a case this project has actually observed.
.PP
The interactive UI's background key
.RB ( b )
re-executes the binary headless in a new session, so transfers survive the
terminal closing.
Exiting the event loop would otherwise kill them, since they are tasks in
the same process.
.
.SH THE hya NAME
.B hya
is the same program under a shorter name. Every install channel \- the
install scripts, Homebrew, the deb, rpm and AUR packages, the macOS installer
and the Windows installer \- puts the CLI on
.B PATH
under both spellings, as a symlink where the platform allows one and as a
second copy where it does not.
.PP
It exists for two reasons. Three letters is less to type for a command run as
often as a download; and
.B hydra
is also the name of THC\-Hydra, the login auditor that Debian, Ubuntu, Kali,
Arch and Homebrew all ship, so a machine can carry two unrelated
.B hydra
programs and resolve whichever comes first on
.BR PATH .
.B hya
is unambiguous.
.PP
Shell completions are name\-bound \- a completion script names the command it
completes \- so the packages install a set for each name. To add the short
name's set by hand:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra install\-completions \-\-bin\-name hya
.fi
.RE
.
